.TH QSORT 3 2026-05-10 "Substrate LibC" "Substrate Library Functions"
.SH NAME
qsort, qsort_r \- sort an array
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RB ( libc ", " -lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.sp
.BI "void qsort(void *" base ", size_t " nmemb ", size_t " size ","
.BI "           int (*" compar ")(const void *, const void *));"
.sp
.BI "void qsort_r(void *" base ", size_t " nmemb ", size_t " size ","
.BI "             int (*" compar ")(const void *, const void *, void *),"
.BI "             void *" arg ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR qsort ()
function sorts an array of
.I nmemb
objects, the initial element of which is pointed to by
.IR base .
The size of each object is specified by
.IR size .
The contents of the array are sorted in ascending order according to a
comparison function pointed to by
.IR compar ,
which is called with two arguments that point to the objects being
compared.
.sp
The comparison function must return an integer less than, equal to, or
greater than zero if the first argument is considered to be respectively
less than, equal to, or greater than the second.  If two members compare
as equal, their order in the sorted array is unspecified.
.sp
.BR qsort_r ()
behaves identically except that the comparison function takes a third
argument,
.IR arg ,
which is forwarded unchanged from the call to
.BR qsort_r ().
This makes it possible to use a single comparator with per-call context
(for example, sort direction, locale, or a key index) without resorting
to a global variable.  Substrate uses the GNU/Linux argument order: the
comparator's signature is
.RI "(const void *" a ", const void *" b ", void *" arg ),
and
.I arg
is the last parameter to
.BR qsort_r ().
.sp
Both functions perform an in-place iterative quicksort with
median-of-three pivot selection and an insertion-sort fallback for small
partitions.  The algorithm is not stable; if stability matters, encode an
original index into the elements and break ties on it inside
.IR compar .
.SH RETURN VALUE
Neither function returns a value.
.SH EXAMPLES
Sort an array of integers in ascending order:
.EX
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static int
cmp_int(const void *a, const void *b)
{
    int ia = *(const int *)a, ib = *(const int *)b;
    return (ia > ib) - (ia < ib);
}

int main(void)
{
    int v[] = { 5, 3, 1, 4, 2 };
    qsort(v, 5, sizeof(int), cmp_int);
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) printf("%d ", v[i]);
    putchar('\\n');
    return 0;
}
.EE
.sp
Sort with caller-supplied direction via
.BR qsort_r ():
.EX
#include <stdlib.h>

static int
cmp_dir(const void *a, const void *b, void *ctx)
{
    int dir = *(int *)ctx;
    int ia = *(const int *)a, ib = *(const int *)b;
    return dir > 0 ? (ia - ib) : (ib - ia);
}

void sort_with(int *v, size_t n, int direction)
{
    qsort_r(v, n, sizeof(int), cmp_dir, &direction);
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bsearch (3),
.BR strcmp (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR qsort ()
conforms to C89, C99, C11, C17, C2x, and POSIX.1-2008.
.BR qsort_r ()
is specified by POSIX.1-2024 and is also available as a GNU and BSD
extension.  Substrate follows the GNU argument order
.RI ( compar ", " arg ),
which matches POSIX.1-2024.
